A **base case** is an input you can answer directly, without another recursive call. A recursive step reduces the current problem to a smaller version of the same problem.

For factorial on nonnegative integers, $0!=1$ is a base case. For a positive integer $n$, use $n!=n(n-1)!$.

```js
function factorial(n) {
  if (!Number.isInteger(n) || n < 0) {
    throw new Error('Use a nonnegative integer');
  }
  if (n === 0) return 1;
  return n * factorial(n - 1);
}
```

Each call decreases n by one. For a valid, reasonably small input, it eventually reaches zero and the return values work their way back up.
